<!-- ============================================================
     ViteVenom / PolinRider npm Supply Chain Detection Rules
     Threat: PolinRider (DPRK-attributed) DEV#POPPER RAT and OmniStealer
             distributed via 7 malicious Tailwind/Vite npm packages using
             TRON and Aptos blockchain dead-drop C2 for XOR key delivery.
             Active June 29 - July 17, 2026.
     Source: opensourcemalware.com ViteVenom analysis (2026-07-19)
             PolinRider / Contagious Interview (G1052, DPRK)
     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1195.001 - Compromise Software Supply Chain (TA0001)
       T1059.007 - Command and Script Interpreter: JavaScript (TA0002)
       T1102.003 - Web Service: One-Way Communication / blockchain C2 (TA0011)
       T1008     - Fallback Channels (Aptos / BSC as TRON fallback) (TA0011)
       T1027     - Obfuscated Files / XOR-encrypted in-memory payload (TA0005)
       T1555.003 - Credentials from Web Browsers (OmniStealer) (TA0006)
       T1552.001 - Credentials in Files - SSH/cloud/npmrc (TA0006)
       T1547.011 - Plist Modification - LaunchAgent persistence (TA0003)
       T1053.005 - Scheduled Task / crontab persistence (TA0003)
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols / Socket.io C2 (TA0011)
     Rule IDs: 104033-104043
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-20

     Campaign Overview - ViteVenom (July 2026):

     PolinRider published 7 malicious npm packages between June 29 and
     July 3 2026, typosquatting Tailwind CSS animation and typography
     libraries used in the React/Vite/Tailwind frontend stack. Each
     package contains a postinstall hook that:

       1. Connects to the TRON blockchain API (api.trongrid.io) and
          queries wallet TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP for the
          latest transaction memo field.
       2. Extracts a base64-encoded XOR key from the memo.
       3. XOR-decrypts an embedded payload blob using that key.
       4. Passes the decrypted JS string to eval() for in-memory execution.
       5. Falls back to Aptos (fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com) if TRON fails.

     The delivered payload is either:
       - DEV#POPPER RAT: Node.js backdoor providing interactive remote
         shell, file system enumeration, and secondary payload delivery
       - OmniStealer: credential harvester targeting Chrome/Firefox/Edge/
         Brave session cookies and login databases, SSH private keys, AWS
         credentials, .npmrc auth tokens, kubeconfig, and .env files

     Malicious packages (all removed from npm registry July 17-19 2026):
       tailwindcss-style-animate 1.1.6
       tailwind-mainanimation 2.3.3
       tailwind-autoanimation 2.3.6
       tailwind-animationbased (multiple versions)
       tailwindcss-typography-style 0.8.2
       tailwindcss-style-modify 0.8.3
       tailwindcss-animate-style 1.2.5

     Known C2 / Blockchain Infrastructure:
       TRON wallet (primary):  TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP
       TRON wallet (fallback): TXfxHUet9pJVU1BgVkBAbrES4YUc1nGzcG
       Aptos address:          0xbe037400670fbf1c32364f762975908dc43eeb38759263e7dfcdabc76380811e
       TRON RPC:               api.trongrid.io
       Aptos RPC:              fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com
       BSC (second fallback):  bsc-dataseed.binance.org
       Socket.io C2 IPs:       166.88.134.62, 198.105.127.210, 23.27.202.27
       (Vercel C2 domains:     already tracked from June 2026 PolinRider campaign)

     File / Package IOC hashes (SHA256):
       9a541dffb7fc18dc71dbc8523ec6c3a71c224ffeb518ae3a8d7d16377aebee58
       bb2a89001410fa5a11dea6477d4f5573130261badc67fe952cfad1174c2f0edd
       7c5adef4b5aee7a4aa6e795a86f8b7d601618c3bc003f1326ca57d03ec7d6524

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rules 104033, 104038:
         - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) -> Wazuh SID 61603
         - Sysmon must capture CommandLine and Image fields.
       Rules 104034, 104035, 104036, 104037:
         - Network/firewall logs -> Wazuh SID 103000 (firewall group anchor)
         - Destination hostname must appear in the raw log; FortiGate,
           pfSense, Suricata, or Sysmon EventID 3 (NetworkConnect) are
           all suitable sources. DNS proxy logs will also trigger these
           rules if they include the destination hostname.
       Rule 104036 (additional):
         - Wazuh FIM syscheck (SID 550-593) on npm node_modules directories
           or developer workspace paths; fires when the malicious package
           JS source containing the wallet address is written to disk.
       Rule 104039:
         - Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) -> Wazuh SID 61644
         - Sysmon must log FileCreate events in AppData browser profile paths.
       Rule 104040:
         - Linux auditd execve+openat syscall rules forwarded to Wazuh.
         - Recommended auditd rule: -a always,exit -F arch=b64
           -S openat -F uid=0 -k credential_access
           (also configure per-user rules for non-root environments)
       Rule 104041:
         - Wazuh FIM (syscheck) with who-data enabled on macOS, monitoring
           /Users/*/Library/LaunchAgents/ and /Library/LaunchAgents/
         - who-data requires macOS Endpoint Security Framework; available
           in Wazuh 4.3+ with 'whodata' mode in ossec.conf syscheck config.
       Rule 104042:
         - Linux auditd execve rules forwarded to Wazuh.
         - Sysmon for Linux EventID 1 also qualifies (if deployed).
       Rule 104043:
         - Frequency rule on 104034; inherits 104034 log source prerequisites.

     Severity tiers:
       11 - Informational FIM new LaunchAgent (possible legitimate software)
       13 - npm/node inline eval execution pattern (high suspicion)
       13 - npm/node attempting crontab or profile modification on Linux
       14 - npm install of known ViteVenom package name (confirmed IOC)
       14 - TRON/Aptos/BSC RPC connection (strong blockchain C2 indicator)
       14 - PolinRider Socket.io C2 IP connection (confirmed C2 IOC)
       14 - OmniStealer browser credential file access from node process
       14 - OmniStealer SSH/cloud credential access from npm/node on Linux
       14 - DEV#POPPER LaunchAgent plist written by npm/node on macOS
       15 - TRON wallet dead-drop address confirmed in network or FIM log
       15 - Sustained TRON C2 beaconing (active DEV#POPPER RAT confirmed)

     References:
       opensourcemalware.com ViteVenom analysis (2026-07-19):
         https://opensourcemalware.com/
       PolinRider / Contagious Interview prior campaign (2026-06-29):
         https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/hijacked-npm-and-go-packages-use-vs.html
       JFrog Security Research - Hijacked npm VSCode Tasks Blockchain:
         https://research.jfrog.com/post/hijacked-npm-vscode-tasks-blockchain/
       MITRE ATT&CK - Contagious Interview G1052:
         https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1052/
       MITRE ATT&CK - T1102.003 Web Service One-Way Communication:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/003/
       MITRE ATT&CK - T1195.001 Compromise Software Dependencies:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/001/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,supply_chain,initial_access,execution,command_and_control,credential_access,persistence,windows,linux,macos,vitevenom,polinrider,dprk,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104033: Known ViteVenom malicious npm package name
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1195.001 (Supply Chain Compromise)

       This rule fires when the npm process is launched with one of the
       7 known malicious ViteVenom package names as a command-line argument.
       All 7 packages were removed from the npm registry July 17-19 2026
       following security community disclosure.

       This fires for direct npm install commands (e.g., "npm install
       tailwindcss-style-animate") and also for npm commands that reference
       a package.json where one of these names appears in the arguments.
       It does NOT reliably fire when the package is pulled as a transitive
       dependency (the package name may not appear in npm's spawned command
       line in that case).

       If this rule fires on a date AFTER July 17 2026, the install was
       attempted against a version no longer in the registry or a cached
       copy; investigate whether the cached package is a genuine old install
       or an adversary replay. Either way, treat the host as potentially
       compromised and audit for OmniStealer or DEV#POPPER artefacts.

       False positives: none expected. These package names are not used by
       any legitimate software. If a developer has a local package that
       accidentally shares one of these names, it would indicate an unusual
       naming collision that warrants investigation regardless.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104033" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\npm(?:\.cmd|\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(tailwindcss-style-animate|tailwind-mainanimation|tailwind-autoanimation|tailwind-animationbased|tailwindcss-typography-style|tailwindcss-style-modify|tailwindcss-animate-style)</field>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: Known malicious Tailwind npm package name detected in npm command on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] -- 7 PolinRider supply chain packages (DEV#POPPER RAT or OmniStealer) delivered as Tailwind CSS typosquats; these packages were removed from the npm registry July 17-19 2026; treat the workstation as compromised and rotate all developer credentials (T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,npm_supply_chain,tailwind_typosquat,ioc_match,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104034: TRON blockchain RPC connection from developer tooling
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1102.003 (Web Service: One-Way Communication)

       ViteVenom's postinstall hook contacts the public TRON blockchain
       API (api.trongrid.io) to read the latest transaction memo from wallet
       TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP. The memo contains the base64-
       encoded XOR key used to decrypt the DEV#POPPER RAT or OmniStealer
       payload blob embedded in the package. This dead-drop mechanism means
       the decryption key can never be sinkholed or blocked without blocking
       the entire TRON blockchain network.

       On corporate endpoints, no legitimate process invoked during software
       development (npm, node, CI runners) has a business reason to query the
       TRON JSON-RPC API. Web3 TRON DApp developers would use this
       legitimately, but the postinstall hook context (npm/node spawned as a
       subprocess of the package manager) distinguishes malicious from
       legitimate: a human TRON developer would invoke their DApp code from
       their own application, not from within a Tailwind/Vite package
       postinstall script.

       False positives: TRON DApp developers using TronWeb in local scripts;
       blockchain engineer CI pipelines that deploy TRON smart contracts.
       Scope with asset groups to exclude designated TRON developer
       workstations if needed.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104034" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>api\.trongrid\.io|fullnode\.tron\.network|api\.tronstack\.io|fullnode\.shasta\.trongrid\.io|rpc\.trongrid\.io</match>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: TRON blockchain RPC connection detected on $(agent.name) -- ViteVenom malicious npm postinstall hooks contact TronGrid public API (api.trongrid.io) to retrieve XOR decryption key from TRON wallet TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP transaction memo; no legitimate npm or CI process needs to query TRON blockchain infrastructure; high-confidence indicator of ViteVenom postinstall hook execution or active DEV#POPPER RAT check-in (T1102.003, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.003</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,blockchain_c2,tron_rpc,command_and_control,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104035: Aptos or BSC blockchain RPC connection (fallback C2)
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1102.003 (Web Service: One-Way Communication), T1008 (Fallback Channels)

       ViteVenom uses a three-chain dead-drop resolver: TRON is the primary
       channel; if the TRON query fails (network block, API outage), the
       postinstall hook falls back to querying the Aptos blockchain wallet
       (fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com) and then Binance Smart Chain (BSC,
       bsc-dataseed.binance.org). This multi-chain fallback makes the C2
       key delivery robust against defenders who block a single blockchain
       RPC endpoint.

       Note on rpc.ankr.com: this endpoint is shared with Solana (Glassworm)
       and Ethereum (EtherHiding/EtherRAT) and may produce false positives.
       The most specific indicators for ViteVenom are the Aptos endpoints
       (fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com, api.mainnet.aptoslabs.com); these
       have virtually no legitimate use on non-Aptos-developer corporate
       endpoints.

       False positives: Aptos DApp developers; BSC (Binance Smart Chain)
       DApp developers. BSC endpoints (bsc-dataseed.binance.org) are also
       queried by crypto portfolio trackers and browser extensions. Scope
       with asset groups for known blockchain developer workstations.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104035" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>fullnode\.mainnet\.aptoslabs\.com|api\.mainnet\.aptoslabs\.com|rpc\.mainnet\.aptos\.dev|rpc\.aptos\.network|bsc-dataseed\.binance\.org|bsc-rpc\.publicnode\.com|bsc-dataseed1\.binance\.org|bsc-dataseed2\.binance\.org</match>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: Aptos or BSC blockchain RPC connection detected on $(agent.name) -- ViteVenom postinstall hooks use Aptos (fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com) and Binance Smart Chain (bsc-dataseed.binance.org) as fallback dead-drop channels when TRON is unreachable; this multi-chain fallback is a DPRK PolinRider fingerprint; developer tooling has no legitimate reason to query Aptos or BSC RPC from package install scripts (T1102.003, T1008)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.003</id>
      <id>T1008</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,blockchain_c2,aptos_bsc_rpc,command_and_control,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104036: TRON wallet dead-drop address IOC in network or FIM log
       Network/firewall logs (SID 103000) or FIM syscheck (SID 550-593)
       MITRE: T1102.003 (Web Service: One-Way Communication)

       The two TRON wallet addresses used by ViteVenom as blockchain C2
       dead drops are confirmed IOCs. They appear in:

         (a) NETWORK logs: as URL path components when the postinstall hook
             queries TronGrid, e.g.:
             GET /v1/accounts/TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP/transactions
             These would appear in HTTP proxy logs, Suricata alerts, or
             Sysmon EventID 3 network logs if deep packet inspection is enabled.

         (b) FIM logs: when Wazuh syscheck scans node_modules and reads the
             malicious package's postinstall script (which contains the wallet
             address as a string literal), the address may appear in the FIM
             alert content for newly-created or modified files.

       Either sighting is a confirmed ViteVenom IOC. FIM detection requires
       syscheck monitoring of node_modules directories or developer workspace
       paths. The SHA256 file hashes (also known ViteVenom IOCs) are not
       matched here to avoid overlap with generic IOC hash rules; add them
       to the iocs/ CSV files for MISP/threat feed integration.

       False positives: essentially none. These wallet addresses are unique
       to the ViteVenom campaign and have no legitimate software use.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104036" level="15">
    <if_sid>103000,550,553,554,593</if_sid>
    <match>TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP|TXfxHUet9pJVU1BgVkBAbrES4YUc1nGzcG</match>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: Confirmed TRON wallet dead-drop address IOC detected on $(agent.name) -- TRON wallets TMfKQEd7TJJa5xNZJZ2Lep838vrzrs7mAP and TXfxHUet9pJVU1BgVkBAbrES4YUc1nGzcG are the primary blockchain C2 dead-drop wallets used by ViteVenom to deliver XOR decryption keys for DEV#POPPER RAT and OmniStealer payloads; any sighting of these addresses in network traffic (TronGrid API requests) or FIM-scanned source files is a confirmed supply chain compromise indicator; isolate host and begin IR (T1102.003, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.003</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,ioc_match,tron_wallet,blockchain_c2,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104037: PolinRider Socket.io C2 server connection
       Network/firewall logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       After the ViteVenom postinstall hook decrypts and evals the
       DEV#POPPER RAT payload, the RAT establishes a persistent Socket.io
       WebSocket connection to one of three known PolinRider C2 servers:
         166.88.134.62
         198.105.127.210
         23.27.202.27

       These are the same C2 IPs documented in the June 2026 PolinRider
       Contagious Interview campaign (html-to-gutenberg / fetch-page-assets
       npm packages). Their reuse in ViteVenom confirms infrastructure
       continuity between the two campaigns. A connection to any of these
       IPs from a developer workstation indicates that the DEV#POPPER RAT
       is running and has successfully established its C2 channel.

       This is a critical-response indicator: the workstation is actively
       under attacker control. Isolate the host immediately, capture volatile
       memory, and escalate to incident response. The developer's npm tokens,
       SSH keys, AWS credentials, and any code repositories they have write
       access to should be treated as compromised.

       False positives: none; these are not CDN, cloud, or shared hosting
       IPs with legitimate multi-tenant use.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104037" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>166\.88\.134\.62|198\.105\.127\.210|23\.27\.202\.27</match>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: Connection to confirmed PolinRider Socket.io C2 server from $(agent.name) -- IPs 166.88.134.62, 198.105.127.210, and 23.27.202.27 are the DEV#POPPER RAT C2 servers from the PolinRider Contagious Interview campaign (DPRK-attributed); an active connection confirms the RAT is running and has established its C2 channel; isolate host immediately, capture memory, and begin incident response (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,ioc_c2,socketio_c2,devpopper_rat,dprk,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104038: node.exe inline eval with XOR/base64 decode pattern
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1027 (Obfuscated Files), T1059.007 (JavaScript)

       When the ViteVenom postinstall hook decrypts its payload and runs it
       via eval(), it typically invokes node with the -e flag (or the long
       form: node -eval / node -e) combined with a decryption stub. The
       commandLine captured by Sysmon EventID 1 would look like:

         node -e "var k=Buffer.from('BASE64KEY','base64');eval(XOR(payload,k))"
         node -e "eval(atob('OBFUSCATED_PAYLOAD'))"
         node -e "require('./_payload')(Buffer.from('KEY','hex'))"

       The pattern of node.exe called with an inline -e/-eval flag containing
       eval(), Buffer.from(), atob(), or String.fromCharCode is a strong
       indicator of in-memory payload execution from a malicious npm hook.
       Legitimate developer use of node -e typically includes debugging
       snippets, not eval(Buffer.from()) patterns.

       This rule can also fire on other npm supply chain attacks that use
       in-memory eval() delivery; it is not exclusive to ViteVenom. Correlate
       with rules 104034-104036 (blockchain RPC connections) on the same host
       within a short window to confirm ViteVenom specifically.

       False positives: rare. Some developer tooling automation scripts use
       node -e for one-liners. Investigate any node -e invocation that
       references eval() combined with base64/hex decoding - legitimate
       automation rarely needs runtime eval of decoded binary content.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104038" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\node(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:(?:-e|--eval)\s+.{0,200}(?:eval\s*\(|Buffer\.from\s*\(|atob\s*\(|String\.fromCharCode\s*\(|fromCharCode)|eval\s*\(\s*(?:require|Buffer|atob|String))</field>
    <description>ViteVenom/PolinRider: node.exe invoked with inline eval or decode pattern on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] -- ViteVenom postinstall hooks execute the XOR-decrypted DEV#POPPER RAT or OmniStealer payload via node -e eval() or --eval with Buffer.from()/atob() decoding; inline eval of decoded binary content is a strong indicator of in-memory supply chain payload execution; correlate with TRON/Aptos C2 connection rules 104034-104035 on this host (T1027, T1059.007)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1027</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,polinrider,eval_execution,fileless_payload,obfuscated_js,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104039: OmniStealer - node.exe accessing browser credential files
       Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644
       MITRE: T1555.003 (Credentials from Web Browsers)

       OmniStealer, the infostealer payload in the majority of ViteVenom
       packages, harvests browser-stored credentials by locating and copying
       the browser credential database files from the victim's AppData path:

         Chrome/Chromium: ...Google\Chrome\User Data\Default\Login Data
                          ...Google\Chrome\User Data\Default\Cookies
                          ...Google\Chrome\User Data\Default\Local State
         Microsoft Edge:  ...Microsoft\Edge\User Data\Default\Login Data
                          ...Microsoft\Edge\User Data\Default\Cookies
         Brave:           ...BraveSoftware\Brave-Browser\User Data\Default\Login Data
         Firefox:         ...Mozilla\Firefox\Profiles\*\logins.json
                          ...Mozilla\Firefox\Profiles\*\key4.db

       Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) fires when node.exe creates a file
       at one of these paths - the most likely scenario is OmniStealer
       creating a temporary copy of the database (since Chrome/Edge lock the
       original file) before reading and exfiltrating its contents. Any file
       creation by node.exe in a browser credential path is highly anomalous
       since no npm package lifecycle step has a legitimate reason to write
       files in browser credential directories.

       False positives: extremely rare. Browser sync tools that integrate
       with Node.js backends would be unusual. Validate the image path:
       legitimate browser utilities run from Program Files, not from npm's
       postinstall context.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104039" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\node(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\AppData\\(?:Local|Roaming)\\(?:Google\\Chrome|Microsoft\\Edge|BraveSoftware\\Brave-Browser|Mozilla\\Firefox\\Profiles\\[^\\]+)\\(?:(?:User\s+Data\\)?[^\\]*\\)?(?:Login\s+Data|Cookies|Local\s+State|Web\s+Data|Network\\Cookies|logins\.json|key4\.db)</field>
    <description>ViteVenom/OmniStealer: node.exe created or copied a browser credential database file on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [file: $(win.eventdata.targetFilename)] -- OmniStealer harvests Chrome, Edge, Brave, and Firefox credential databases (Login Data, Cookies, logins.json, key4.db) by copying them from the user profile; node.exe has no legitimate reason to create files in browser credential directories; this is a confirmed OmniStealer collection indicator (T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,omnistealer,credential_theft,browser_credentials,collection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104040: OmniStealer - npm/node accessing SSH/cloud credentials
       Linux auditd syscall events
       MITRE: T1552.001 (Credentials in Files), T1555.003

       On Linux (and macOS with auditd), OmniStealer accesses a broad set
       of developer credential files to harvest authentication secrets:
         ~/.ssh/id_rsa, id_ed25519, id_ecdsa, id_dsa (SSH private keys)
         ~/.aws/credentials, ~/.aws/config (AWS CLI credentials)
         ~/.npmrc (npm authentication tokens for private registries)
         ~/.kube/config (Kubernetes cluster access credentials)
         ~/.docker/config.json (Docker Hub and registry credentials)
         ~/.config/gcloud/credentials (Google Cloud SDK credentials)
         ~/.netrc (FTP/HTTP credentials in plaintext)
         .env files (application-level secrets and API keys)
         ~/.pypirc (PyPI upload credentials)

       When npm/node opens any of these paths during package installation
       or postinstall hook execution, it is almost certainly OmniStealer
       harvesting credentials rather than a legitimate package install step.
       npm install never has a legitimate reason to access SSH private keys,
       cloud credentials, or auth tokens stored in ~/.npmrc outside of its
       own npm-authentication context (the .npmrc access during install is to
       READ registry tokens, not to exfiltrate them).

       Requires auditd with openat syscall logging. To generate these events:
         auditctl -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S openat -k cred_access

       False positives: developer tools that legitimately use cloud credentials
       during postinstall (e.g., Terraform providers that run during install).
       Known-good postinstall scripts that access ~/.aws/ for build artifact
       retrieval may trigger this. Review and allowlist specific processes after
       confirming legitimacy in your environment.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104040" level="14">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="audit.syscall">^open(at)?$</field>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">(?:^|/)(?:node|npm|npx)$</field>
    <match>\.ssh/id_rsa|\.ssh/id_ed25519|\.ssh/id_ecdsa|\.ssh/id_dsa|\.aws/credentials|\.npmrc|\.kube/config|\.docker/config\.json|\.netrc|gcloud/credentials|\.pypirc</match>
    <description>ViteVenom/OmniStealer: npm or node process opened a sensitive credential file on $(agent.name) [process: $(audit.exe)] -- OmniStealer targets SSH private keys, AWS credentials, .npmrc auth tokens, kubeconfig, Docker credentials, and GCP credentials for exfiltration; npm or node opening these files during package install context is a strong OmniStealer credential harvest indicator; check for concurrent TRON/Aptos C2 connections (rules 104034-104035) to confirm ViteVenom (T1552.001, T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,omnistealer,linux,credential_theft,ssh_keys,cloud_credentials,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104041: DEV#POPPER RAT - LaunchAgent plist created by npm/node
       Wazuh FIM syscheck new file (SID 554) with who-data
       MITRE: T1547.011 (Plist Modification - LaunchAgent persistence)

       On macOS, DEV#POPPER RAT establishes persistence by writing a
       LaunchAgent property list file to one of:
         ~/Library/LaunchAgents/*.plist  (user-level, survives user login)
         /Library/LaunchAgents/*.plist   (system-level, requires root)

       The postinstall hook creates the plist from within the npm/node
       process tree, so if Wazuh FIM is configured with who-data enabled
       on macOS (requires Endpoint Security Framework; Wazuh 4.3+), the
       creating process (node or npm) can be attributed directly.

       The plist typically points to a long-running node.exe invocation or
       shell script that reconnects to the PolinRider C2 on login. The plist
       ServiceName in ViteVenom variants observed to date mimics system
       service names (com.apple.sysupdate, com.apple.launchservices.sync, etc.)
       to blend into the LaunchAgent list.

       Requires Wazuh syscheck with whodata=yes monitoring:
         /Users/*/Library/LaunchAgents
         /Library/LaunchAgents

       Without who-data, new plist files in LaunchAgents will still fire
       SID 554 but the process attribution field (syscheck.audit.process.name)
       will be absent and the rule will not match.

       False positives: legitimate software (package managers like Homebrew,
       app installers) does create LaunchAgents during installation. Tune by
       allowlisting known-good plist names in your environment. The key
       differentiator for DEV#POPPER is the creating process being node/npm.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104041" level="14">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <match>/Library/LaunchAgents/</match>
    <field name="syscheck.path" type="pcre2">(?i)/Library/LaunchAgents/.*\.plist$</field>
    <field name="syscheck.audit.process.name" type="pcre2">(?i)^(node|npm|npx)$</field>
    <description>ViteVenom/DEV#POPPER RAT: New LaunchAgent plist created by node or npm process on $(agent.name) [file: $(syscheck.path), creator: $(syscheck.audit.process.name)] -- DEV#POPPER RAT establishes macOS persistence by writing a LaunchAgent plist to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ from an npm postinstall hook; a LaunchAgent created by node or npm is a confirmed DEV#POPPER persistence mechanism; remove the plist, kill any running node processes, and begin full host investigation (T1547.011, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.011</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,devpopper_rat,macos,launchagent,persistence,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104042: DEV#POPPER RAT - crontab or profile modification by npm/node
       Linux auditd execve - Wazuh decoded_as auditd
       MITRE: T1053.005 (Scheduled Task/Job: Cron), T1546.004 (Unix Shell Config)

       On Linux, DEV#POPPER RAT establishes persistence by modifying the
       user's crontab or by appending a loader command to shell profile
       scripts (.bashrc, .zshrc, .profile, .bash_profile). This is done
       from within the npm postinstall hook context (node or npm as the
       parent process).

       Observed persistence commands from the PolinRider / Contagious
       Interview campaign:
         crontab -l | { cat; echo "*/5 * * * * node /tmp/.update"; } | crontab -
         echo 'node /tmp/.cache/.update &' >> ~/.bashrc
         echo 'node /tmp/.config/update &' >> ~/.profile

       The key behavioral signature is crontab(1) or tee/echo being invoked
       as a child of npm or node during package installation. Legitimate npm
       packages do not modify system crontabs or user shell profiles.

       This rule also catches sed/awk usage against profile files from npm
       context, as some variants use in-place file editing for profile
       modification.

       False positives: virtually none. Crontab modification during npm
       install is not a legitimate package management operation.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104042" level="13">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="audit.syscall">execve</field>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">(?:^|/)(?:node|npm|npx)$</field>
    <field name="audit.cmd" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:^|/)(?:crontab|tee|sed|awk|perl|python3?)\s|(?:\.bashrc|\.zshrc|\.profile|\.bash_profile|\.bash_login|\.xprofile)\b</field>
    <description>ViteVenom/DEV#POPPER RAT: npm or node process spawned crontab/shell-profile modifier on $(agent.name) [cmd: $(audit.cmd)] -- DEV#POPPER RAT establishes Linux persistence by modifying crontab or appending loader commands to .bashrc/.zshrc/.profile from within an npm postinstall hook; crontab or profile modification from npm context has no legitimate use case; isolate host and check for concurrent TRON blockchain C2 activity (T1053.005, T1546.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1546.004</id>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>vitevenom,devpopper_rat,linux,crontab_persistence,profile_modification,persistence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104043: Sustained ViteVenom TRON C2 beaconing
